    "answer": "The h1\u2192h5 transfer problem is real and is being caused by the **s2\u2013s3 segment**, not by loss of basic connectivity.\n\n**Evidence**\n- h1\u2192h5 TCP test: only **131 KB sent in 5 s** (~**0.21 Mbit/s**), with **8 retransmissions**; h5\u2019s receiver reported no completed payload.\n- h1\u2192h5 ICMP succeeds with no loss, but RTT is extremely high and variable: **76\u2013302 ms**, **162 ms average**.\n- Installed forwarding sends traffic for h5 from:\n  - **s1 port 1 \u2192 s2**\n  - **s2 port 1 \u2192 s3**\n  - so h1\u2192h5 is taking the long ring path through s2/s3/s4 rather than the direct active **s1\u2013s5** link.\n- Adjacent-path probes isolate the impairment:\n  - h1\u2192h2: **1.65 ms average RTT**\n  - **h2\u2192h3: 359.5 ms average RTT** (214\u2013472 ms)\n  - h3\u2192h4: **1.33 ms average RTT**\n- A direct h2\u2192h3 TCP test is likewise limited to about **0.19 Mbit/s**.\n\n**Diagnosis**\nThe active link between **s2 port 1 and s3 port 2** has severe delay (and likely impairment that prevents TCP from growing normally). h1\u2192h5 is badly affected because its installed path traverses that link. The direct s1\u2013s5 link is active, but the present forwarding rules do not use it for h5-destined traffic.\n\n**Next step**\nInspect the s2:1\u2194s3:2 link\u2019s configuration/impairment and its queue/drop state, then correct the forwarding policy/path so h1\u2192h5 can use s1\u2013s5 if that is the intended low-latency route.",
